"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[18556],{218556:e=>{e.exports='# Databricks Environment Management\n\n## Critical Rules\n\n### Package Installation\n\n1. **ALWAYS use `%pip install`** — never `conda install`, never `!pip install`.\n   - `%pip` is notebook-scoped and the officially supported method on Databricks.\n   - `conda install` can corrupt the base environment and break the cluster.\n   - `!pip install` does not properly integrate with the notebook session.\n\n2. **NEVER modify `pyproject.toml`, `setup.py`, `setup.cfg`, or `requirements.txt`** to install dependencies.\n   - Use `%pip install` directly in a notebook cell instead.\n   - Editing project files is unnecessarily complex and error-prone in the Databricks notebook context.\n\n3. **NEVER suggest creating virtualenvs, modifying `PYTHONPATH`, or modifying `sys.path`**.\n   - These can make the entire cluster unusable and break the Databricks runtime environment.\n\n### Core Packages — Do Not Touch\n\n4. **NEVER suggest changing, downgrading, or upgrading core packages**, including but not limited to:\n   - `pyspark`, `delta-spark`, `mlflow`, `databricks-sdk`, `databricks-connect`\n   - `py4j`, `pyarrow`, `pandas` (runtime-bundled version), `numpy` (runtime-bundled version)\n   - These are version-locked to the Databricks Runtime (DBR). Overriding them causes data corruption, silent correctness bugs, and cluster instability.\n   - This includes pinning core packages to specific versions via `%pip install` — even to "keep" them at their current version. Do not include core packages in any `%pip install` command.\n   - When a non-core package pulls in incompatible core package versions as transitive dependencies, the fix is to find a version of the **non-core** package that is compatible with the existing core packages — not to pin or override the core packages.\n\n### Runtime and Compute — Do Not Change\n\n5. **NEVER suggest upgrading or downgrading the Python version on DBR.**\n   - The Python version is fixed per DBR version. It cannot be changed without changing the entire runtime.\n   - Instead, work within the current Python version — suggest compatible package versions or alternative libraries.\n\n6. **NEVER suggest downgrading or upgrading DBR/MLR versions** to fix a library issue.\n   - This is disruptive (requires cluster recreation), rarely solves the actual library issue, and may break other workloads.\n   - Do not mention changing DBR/MLR as an option anywhere in your response — not as a primary solution, not as a long-term solution, not as a secondary option, not in a list of alternatives. Omit it entirely.\n   - If the user explicitly asks "should I upgrade MLR/DBR?", answer **no** and redirect to `%pip install`.\n   - The correct long-term solution for library version needs is `%pip install package==version` in a notebook cell — this is persistent (the cell re-runs each session), portable, and non-disruptive.\n\n7. **NEVER suggest switching between cluster and serverless compute types** to resolve package installation or dependency issues — not as a workaround, not as a fallback, not even if the package cannot be installed on the current compute type.\n   - Switching compute types does not fix library problems and is out of scope for environment management.\n   - Instead, suggest alternatives that work within the user\'s current compute type: finding a pre-built wheel, using a pure-Python alternative package, or checking if a compatible version exists.\n\n### Deprecated Practices\n\n8. **NEVER recommend DBFS usage** for storing or installing libraries.\n   - DBFS is deprecated in favor of Unity Catalog Volumes.\n   - DBFS has security and governance gaps and is unsupported on newer access modes.\n\n---\n\n## Environment Guidance — What to Recommend\n\n### Notebook-Scoped Installation (All Compute Types)\n\n`%pip install` is the primary method for installing packages in notebooks:\n\n```python\n%pip install <package-name>\n```\n\n**Key behavior:**\n\n- Notebook-scoped: only affects the current session\'s Python environment\n- The `%pip install` cell stays in the notebook and re-executes automatically when the notebook runs, so packages are effectively available every session\n- This is the **standard approach for package persistence** — keeping `%pip install` in a notebook cell is the recommended way to ensure packages are always available\n- Supported on both classic clusters and serverless compute\n- Place `%pip` commands at the top of the notebook, before any other code\n- After `%pip install`, the Python process is automatically restarted — any variables or imports from prior cells are lost\n\n### Classic Clusters — Cluster Libraries (Secondary Option)\n\nCluster libraries are an additional option on classic compute for cluster-wide sharing:\n\n- Installed via the Databricks UI (Compute → Libraries tab)\n- Available to all notebooks attached to the cluster\n- Persist across cluster restarts\n- Support PyPI packages, wheel files, JARs, and Maven coordinates\n\nCluster libraries are useful when the user explicitly needs the same package across many notebooks on the same cluster. However, **always recommend `%pip install` in a notebook cell first** — it is the standard, portable approach. Only mention cluster libraries as a secondary option if the user specifically asks about cluster-wide sharing.\n\n### Serverless Compute — Limited Options\n\nOn serverless compute:\n\n- **Only `%pip install` and the Environment panel are supported**\n- Do NOT suggest cluster libraries, init scripts, or cluster environment variables — these are not available on serverless\n- The **Environment panel** (in the notebook UI sidebar) allows managing dependencies for serverless notebooks\n- If a package requires native compilation (C/C++ extensions) and fails to build on serverless, suggest finding a pre-built wheel or a pure-Python alternative — do NOT suggest switching to classic compute\n\n### Resolving Dependency Conflicts\n\nWhen the user encounters dependency conflicts:\n\n1. Check which packages are pre-installed on the current DBR version\n2. Suggest compatible versions that work with the runtime\'s existing packages\n3. Never suggest overriding core packages (see rule #4 above)\n4. Use `%pip install package==version` with a specific compatible version\n5. If a conflict is truly unresolvable, explain the constraint clearly rather than suggesting runtime changes\n\n### Common Scenarios — Correct Responses\n\n**User asks "Should I upgrade MLR/DBR to get a newer version of X?"**\n→ Answer: No. Use `%pip install X==<desired-version>` to install the version you need within your current runtime. This is both the immediate and long-term solution — the `%pip install` cell persists in the notebook and re-runs each session. Do not mention MLR/DBR upgrades anywhere in your response.\n\n**User has a package that won\'t build/install on serverless**\n→ Suggest: (1) finding a pre-built wheel for the package, (2) using a pure-Python alternative, or (3) pinning to a version that ships pre-built wheels for the platform. Do NOT suggest switching to classic compute.\n\n**User gets `ERROR_CORE_PACKAGE_VERSION_CHANGE` or a core package conflict from a transitive dependency**\n→ The problem is that the package being installed requires newer versions of core packages (e.g., numpy, packaging). Find an older version of the **non-core** package that is compatible with the runtime\'s existing core packages. Do NOT pin or override the core packages in `%pip install`.\n\n**User asks about a pre-installed package version mismatch**\n→ Use `%pip install package==version` to install the specific non-core package version needed. Do NOT suggest changing the runtime version. Do NOT pin core packages.\n'}}]);
